{"name":"Bullet Hell","hex":"#faf1c8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bullet hell","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A (genre of) two-dimensional shoot 'em up in which the playfield is almost filled with a barrage of enemy bullets."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bullet_hell"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bullet hell","blocks":[{"text":"Bullet hell , also known as manic shooter, is a subgenre of shoot 'em up video games with large amounts of projectiles the player is required to dodge. Introduced in 1993 with Batsugun and initially limited to vertically scrolling shooters, bullet hell elements were later added to other genres. The large number of projectiles is usually balanced by the player ship being treated as a single point for collision detection."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Danmaku_in_Taisei_Project.png/330px-Danmaku_in_Taisei_Project.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullet_hell","wikidata":"Q821157"},{"title":"Bullet","blocks":[{"text":"A bullet is a type of projectile that is shot from the barrel of a firearm. Bullets are made of a variety of materials, including copper, lead, steel, polymer, rubber and even wax. They do not normally contain explosives, but are kinetic projectiles that strike or damage the target by transferring kinetic energy upon impact and penetration. They are made in shapes and constructions that vary for different applications, including specialized functions such as hunting, target shooting, combat, and training. They are often tapered, making them more aerodynamic. Bullet size is expressed by weight and diameter in both imperial and metric measurement systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/GP11_coupe.JPG/330px-GP11_coupe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullet","wikidata":"Q174583"},{"title":"Hell","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, Hell is a location or state in the afterlife in which souls are subjected to punishment after death. Religions with a linear divine history sometimes depict hells as eternal, such as in some versions of Christianity and Islam, whereas religions with reincarnation usually depict a hell as an intermediary period between incarnations, as is the case in the Indian religions. Religions typically locate Hell in another dimension or under Earth's surface. Other afterlife destinations include heaven, paradise, purgatory, limbo, and the underworld."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Fra_Angelico_010.jpg/330px-Fra_Angelico_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell","wikidata":"Q564"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Noiz2sa_-_little_hard.png/500px-Noiz2sa_-_little_hard.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Noiz2sa_-_little_hard.png/500px-Noiz2sa_-_little_hard.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Noiz2sa - little hard","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANoiz2sa_-_little_hard.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/MaryRose-round_shot.JPG/500px-MaryRose-round_shot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/MaryRose-round_shot.JPG/1280px-MaryRose-round_shot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/MaryRose-round_shot.JPG/500px-MaryRose-round_shot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Round shot from the 16th century 